    {
      "id": 12,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        -40,
        -1020
      ],
      "size": [
        940,
        960
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 11,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Note",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# TEST: EPS Model / CLIP / VAE Switchers - and the trap they set\n\nThese three do for a single MODEL, CLIP or VAE what EPS Image Switcher does for\npictures: wire several in, tick the ones you want, and the workflow runs once\nper ticked input.\n\n**This one needs your own checkpoints.** Two Checkpoint Loaders are wired up\nwith nothing chosen - the red-titled nodes on the left. Pick a file in each,\nthen Run.\n\n## Pass 1 - two models, one prompt\n\nBoth loaders feed all three switchers. With both rows ticked you get two\npictures, one per checkpoint, everything else identical.\n\n## Pass 2 - the free lunch\n\nUntick row 2 on all three switchers and Run. One picture - and **checkpoint B\nnever loads at all**. Not \"loads and then gets thrown away\": the whole branch\nabove a switched-off input never executes. Watch the canvas during the run\nand loader B never lights up. A switched-off branch costs nothing, so leaving\noptions wired in and turned off is a perfectly good way to work.\n\nUntick everything and Run: the queue still succeeds and writes nothing. That\nis a legal state too.\n\n## Pass 3 - the trap. Read this one.\n\nSay you tick 3 models on the Model Switcher and 2 VAEs on the VAE Switcher.\nHow many runs do you get?\n\n**Three. Not six.**\n\nComfyUI lines lists up side by side and repeats the shorter one's last entry:\n\n    run 1:  model 1 + vae 1\n    run 2:  model 2 + vae 2\n    run 3:  model 3 + vae 2   <- vae 2 again, not a new combination\n\nOne switcher is one axis. Two switchers do **not** multiply. If you want\nevery model against every VAE, that is what **EPS Run Multiplier** is for:\nwire `models` -> `model` and `vaes` -> `vae` and its `sweep_mode: multiply`\n(the default) crosses the two lists on purpose - all six.\n(`eps-test-run-multiplier.json` demos the other multiply, images x prompts.)\n\n## Which switcher should you actually be using?\n\nIf your model, CLIP and VAE all come out of the same checkpoint file - which\nis the normal case - **use EPS Checkpoint Switcher instead**. One node, one\ntick per file, and the three outputs cannot come apart. There is a demo next\ndoor: `eps-test-checkpoint-switcher.json`.\n\nThese three are for when the pieces come from *different* places: an\nexternal VAE, a swapped text encoder, a model that arrives through a loader\nchain rather than a plain checkpoint. That is the job they are good at, and\nthis graph is deliberately the simplest version of it so you can see the\nwiring.\n\n## Housekeeping, same on all three\n\n- Wire the bottom socket and a new empty one appears below it. You never go\n  hunting in a menu to add an input.\n- The box in the header toggles every row at once, and shows a dash when\n  they are mixed.\n- Double-click a row to give it a real name. Display only - wires and toggles\n  do not care what it is called.\n- Unwiring does not take a socket away again, so a name you typed cannot\n  vanish underneath you.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 13,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        0,
        340
      ],
      "size": [
        300,
